A||||13||Jefrysuko||Speaker sudgestions wanted||||1||
Z||000000||Jefrysuko||09-25-2001||10:53 PM||||Hello all.  I was browsing crutchfield today and came across these<BR> <IMG SRC="http://pix.crutchfield.com/products/2001/108/l108402cs.jpeg"> <BR>Infinity Kappa 40.2cs <BR>4" Component Speaker System <BR>Save $100! Was $249.95, Now: $149.95<BR> <BR>Sounds like a good deal to me.  I would like to put two set's of these im my new SE, both front and rear (yeah I got the idea from Orief).  My questions are about the speakers.  The crossover says that it is a two way passive crossover system.  If I remember right this means that their is a "high pass" for the tweeter and a "low pass" for the woofer right?  Now here is my main question.  Will this "low pass" circuit filter out the high frequencies meant for the tweeter or the the ones that are two low for the tiny woofer to handle?<P>The reason I ask is because I plan on running them straight off the deck for awhile along with the stock sub.  I don't want to have to run some sort of crossover to filter out the extreme low frequencies.  Thanks <BR>Jeffrey<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Philphine||09-25-2001||11:11 PM||||i like infinity's myself. the included x-over is to separate the freqs bettween the tweeter and mid. you'll still need something to keep the bass and/or sub bass away from the 4". cheapest is bass blockers (caps inline with speaker leads) up to a separate active x-over. if you keep building the system you can make those decisions later.<P>i spoke too soon. i've never had the stock sub system to know what kind of x-over you might have already. if there isn't one what i said will work.<P>------------------<BR>Phil T.<P>start where you are,<BR>use what you have,<BR>do what you can.<p>[This message has been edited by Philphine (edited 09-25-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||09-26-2001||12:57 AM||||The crossover for Infinity's crosses at 5000hz. That means all freq's 10 to 5000 go to the woofer and 5000 to 22000 go to the tweeter. You don't need to cut out the lower bass freq's just keep the bass on the stereo set low. The Factory sub has it's own gain for the sub so you will still get bass out of it. If you want to use a low freq cut-off, A set of passive "BassBlockers" at the 100hz would be the best choice since the woofers are capable down to 80hz. <P>If you need help installing the tweeters, I have the measurements and paint to match the panels. I can also show you how the make the mounts so the 4" woofers fit in the sail panel.<P>------------------<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">     <BR><B><I>Got Emblems?</I></B><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 09-26-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Jefrysuko||09-26-2001||10:11 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Oreif:<BR><B>If you need help installing the tweeters, I have the measurements and paint to match the panels. I can also show you how the make the mounts so the 4" woofers fit in the sail panel.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Thanks Orief,<BR>Do you have paint for a tan interior?  I saw a picture, in another post, of how you made the 4" woofers mount in the stock place, that is what made me want to do this.  If you have anything else you can show me I would love to see it.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Oreif||09-26-2001||10:22 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Jefrysuko:<BR><B> Thanks Orief,<BR>Do you have paint for a tan interior?  I saw a picture, in another post, of how you made the 4" woofers mount in the stock place, that is what made me want to do this.  If you have anything else you can show me I would love to see it.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I have the tan but for the 88 beechwood I have to mix 2 colors. So just let me know which you need.<BR>I have all the stuff required to make the adapters fir the sail panels and the plates that fill in the 4X10 holes too. You can get the brown speaker grill cloth at Radio Shack to recover the front grills if they are faded and worn.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Jefrysuko||09-26-2001||03:35 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Oreif:<BR><B> I have the tan but for the 88 beechwood I have to mix 2 colors. So just let me know which you need.<BR>I have all the stuff required to make the adapters fir the sail panels and the plates that fill in the 4X10 holes too. You can get the brown speaker grill cloth at Radio Shack to recover the front grills if they are faded and worn.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I only need the tan right now.  I would like to do the same thing in my 88 GT eventually but think I will try and find something that dosn't involve any cutting.<P>I was just planning on using 1/4" hardboard that I have laying around from an old project for the adapters so I think I have that covered.<P>I actually just went and did a search and saw that you put 5 1/4" components in the front of your old car.  Would you recomend going with those in front and the 4" in back or is there some reason not to do this?<BR>Thanks<BR>Jeffrey<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Alex4mula||09-26-2001||04:03 PM||||Those are great speakers. 4" ar ok for the back but I would put 5 1/4 in front. You have the space for it and price shouldn't be too much higher. Will give you a little better bass response. I have 5 1/4 components there and love them.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Spektrum-87GT||09-26-2001||08:32 PM||||unless you want superb high quality sound... go with pioneer speakers, cheap and high quality(although not as high as infinity) 150 for 2 4" speakers is an insane price, i dont care how good they sound. Another tip... never buy from crutchfield, they do have great customer service, but you can find them elsewhere for up to 1/3 of the price||||reg||1
Z||000008||Oreif||09-26-2001||08:54 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Jefrysuko:<BR><B> <P>I was just planning on using 1/4" hardboard that I have laying around from an old project for the adapters so I think I have that covered.<P>I actually just went and did a search and saw that you put 5 1/4" components in the front of your old car.  Would you recomend going with those in front and the 4" in back or is there some reason not to do this?<BR>Thanks<BR>Jeffrey<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>1/4" is too thick. You need to use the plastic plates that are already used and make a thin plate to mount the speakers. The sail panel frame is not flat. If you bolt a piece of wood to it the speakers will be angled towards the rear window.<P> I have never had 5 1/4" speakers in my First Fiero but I have installed them in other peoples Fiero's. I went with the 4" seperates all around because of the set-up I am using.<P><BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Spektrum-87GT:<BR><B> <P>unless you want superb high quality sound... go with pioneer speakers, cheap and high quality(although not as high as infinity) 150 for 2 4" speakers is an insane price, i dont care how good they sound. Another tip... never buy from crutchfield, they do have great customer service, but you can find them elsewhere for up to 1/3 of the price<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Component speakers always sound better than multi-driver speakers. Pioneer is a good "replacement" speaker. As for the price, The Infinity's at $149 is a very good price. I checked the other 6 places that I get speakers from and the cheapest was $159.<BR>You can always beat Crutchfield's normal low prices easily. But some of their sales stuff is hard to beat. Also it's $149 for 2 4" woofers, 2 1" tweeters AND crossovers.<p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 09-26-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||Jefrysuko||09-27-2001||01:02 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Oreif:<BR><B><BR>1/4" is too thick. You need to use the plastic plates that are already used and make a thin plate to mount the speakers. The sail panel frame is not flat. If you bolt a piece of wood to it the speakers will be angled towards the rear window.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Well it looks as if I could use your help then.  I know those frames are a little hard to work with, since you seem to have it all worked out that would probably save me a bunch of time and agrivation.  I am actually going to be in your area the 5th and 6th of October if you are going to be around I would like to possibly get those and the paint.<BR>Thanks for all the help.<BR>Jeffrey<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000010||Jefrysuko||09-27-2001||01:17 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Alex4mula:<BR><B>Those are great speakers. 4" ar ok for the back but I would put 5 1/4 in front. You have the space for it and price shouldn't be too much higher. Will give you a little better bass response. I have 5 1/4 components there and love them.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Actually the price is quite a bit different.  They are out of the "Infinity Kappa 50.2cs" now they have the "Infinity Kappa 50.3cs" which run $279.95.  Does anyone have a good place to get the 50.2's still?<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Spektrum-87GT:<BR><B>unless you want superb high quality sound... go with pioneer speakers, cheap and high quality(although not as high as infinity) 150 for 2 4" speakers is an insane price, i dont care how good they sound. Another tip... never buy from crutchfield, they do have great customer service, but you can find them elsewhere for up to 1/3 of the price</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yeah I do want a little higher quality that the pioneers offer.  I have Pioneers in my 88 GT and they sound alright the only reason I have them though is because I am afraid to cut anything in that car.  I am always leery of Crutchfield also that is one of the reasons I started this post.  I have been looking around though and this is the cheapest I have found for those speakers.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000011||Fie Ro||09-27-2001||04:54 PM||||Well I have a very clean system (higher points on clarity than most big $$$ Boom Boom  systems!). I made a custom sub for the console fitted with a Boston Acoustics RS8" 300W sub (special for small enclosures!) I have a JVC digifine 4" composet up front (can handle 150Watt). those are powered by a Magnat amp. frequenties below 100hz are all send to the sub, a 4" composet can't handle that at high volumes. This will really give a clean and tight sound without rattling speakers...still have the original speakers in the back running from my headunit (they don't interfere!). Main thing with a good system is to get the EQ balance right...DO listen to your ears and not only to the bass! I made adjustments to the crossovers and amp to get the front speakers and sub balanced (ie to give them the frequenties they're good at at different volumes). Then I use my headunit to make some different EQ settings depending on musicstyle/mood ;-)<BR>Including Boston sub, JVC composet, 4 channel amp, pioneer headunit, 6CD changer and wiring I spend less then $800 total and this is a real clean system compared to many others....you don't need 12" twin subs in a fiero unless you're deaf ;-)<P>I have looked for original 4"x10" speakers but they're hard to get here and they do not have enough power...hope this gives some inspiration ;-)<P>------------------<BR>-The Beauty & The Beast-<BR>1987 Pontiac Fiero GT & <BR>1977 Dodge W200||||reg||6
Z||000012||Jefrysuko||09-28-2001||02:34 AM||||Thanks guy's<BR>I am going to start with the 4" components for now and think a little on the front.  I will probably end up going 5 1/4 but don't exactly have the money to do it all right now anyways.  I figure since I havn't cleaned up this car yet I will clean the carpets and redo my falling headliner while I have the interior pulled apart for the speakers.  That should give me time to save some cash and make sure what I want to do.  I also discovered that my distributor o-ring is leaking and my alternator has started to squeek (dry bearing) so I have a little work to do here before it gets to cold.<BR>Thanks for all your help.<BR>Jeffrey||||reg||1
Z||000013||Bill Shenefelt||12-25-2001||07:56 AM||||More of another question than a reply, but....<BR>I had a pair of 6.5 inch Diamond component speakers driven by a kicker amp, combined with a 10 inch sub in my 86 on a second kicker amp.  Sounded great, but had to use enclosures for all three behind the seats to do it.  I doubt that the sound could be beat.  Took up a lot of leg room (nearly 8 inches of seat forward movement). <BR> I have the 6.5 inch (uses a separate aluminum dome tweeter) and also a pr of one way 4.5 inch Diamonds speakers(had them in the front dash locations-no added value though) and was wondering if there was a good place for the speakers to perform-doors don't seem to work for me)  I also have a pr of Boston and a pr of Infinity 4 inch 2 way plate speakers.  Tried both in my 88 in the rear panels instead of the ovals that were stock, but really did not carry any decent impact.  The 6.5s are THE speakers and I want to use them if possible.  Anyone ever cut thru the firewall for an infinite baffle?  any other suggestions?  Saw a post bo Olef but no photos or detailed descriptions of the mounting.  I heard a (stereo store) setup using the same components with the custom made fiberglass front floor-side baffle enclosures-garbage!<BR>Any good locations or techniques?<BR>Bill Shenefelt ||||reg||1
